00:12:19.471 --> 00:12:20.872
want to keep track of it.

00:12:20.872 --> 00:12:22.708
There's 2 orbitals in there.

00:12:22.708 --> 00:12:25.510
You didn't fill 1 orbital
with 4 electrons, right?

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:31.616
So this would be pi 2px
and pi 2py 4, right?

00:12:31.616 --> 00:12:33.351
That's what's
happening in there.

00:12:33.351 --> 00:12:34.319
OK.

00:12:34.319 --> 00:12:36.321
But the other thing you
can see from the bot--

00:12:36.321 --> 00:12:39.357
from this filling is,
also, the bond order.

00:12:39.357 --> 00:12:41.493
And so for the bond order--

00:12:41.493 --> 00:12:48.266
bond order, here you
would have 1/2 times,

00:12:48.266 --> 00:12:49.701
and it's all of the--

00:12:49.701 --> 00:12:52.604
remember, the bonder is all
of the bonding electrons

00:12:52.604 --> 00:12:55.607
minus antibonding electrons
divided by 2, right.

00:12:55.607 --> 00:12:58.143
And if it's zero, it's
not a stable molecule.

00:12:58.143 --> 00:12:59.778
If it's greater than
zero, it's stable.

00:12:59.778 --> 00:13:02.914
And the higher the bond order,
the higher the bond strength.

00:13:02.914 --> 00:13:04.916
So that's also from Friday.

00:13:04.916 --> 00:13:13.024
And so this is 2 minus 2 plus
4 minus 2, and so you get 2.

00:13:13.024 --> 00:13:13.525
Nope.

00:13:13.525 --> 00:13:15.727
I missed a 2.

00:13:15.727 --> 00:13:20.665
Plus 2, plus 4 minus 2.

00:13:20.665 --> 00:13:23.668
2 minus 2, that kind
of cancels out, right,

00:13:23.668 --> 00:13:28.907
plus 2, plus 4, minus
2, all divided by 2.

00:13:28.907 --> 00:13:33.378
So the bonder for oxy--
now, the bonder for No--

00:13:33.378 --> 00:13:42.320
bond order for No
is going to be 2.5.

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:44.689
Well, that's kind of
interesting, now, you can see--

00:13:44.689 --> 00:13:45.190
OK.

00:13:45.190 --> 00:13:47.592
So No is stronger--

00:13:47.592 --> 00:13:50.695
more strongly bound than O2,
less strongly bound than N2.

00:13:50.695 --> 00:13:51.963
And it's kind of interesting.

00:13:51.963 --> 00:13:54.399
How can I make No a stronger--

00:13:54.399 --> 00:13:56.334
a more strongly bound molecule?

00:13:56.334 --> 00:13:59.237
Well, I can take
an electron away.

00:13:59.237 --> 00:14:01.506
It's a little bit
counterintuitive, right?

00:14:01.506 --> 00:14:07.312
Because for No
plus, the bond order

00:14:07.312 --> 00:14:10.515
goes up because the
electron that I'd taken away

00:14:10.515 --> 00:14:12.717
was in an antibonding orbital.

00:14:12.717 --> 00:14:13.585
Right.

00:14:13.585 --> 00:14:16.888
So No plus actually has
a stronger bond than No,

00:14:16.888 --> 00:14:18.423
even though it has
one less electron.

00:14:18.423 --> 00:14:21.526
You get this all from just
looking at which electrons

00:14:21.526 --> 00:14:24.863
are occupying which orbitals.

00:14:24.863 --> 00:14:29.734
In this case, you had in this
very much not drawn to scale--

00:14:29.734 --> 00:14:33.805
oh, boy-- figure, right.

00:14:33.805 --> 00:14:37.642
What you had was one up there.

00:14:37.642 --> 00:14:40.745
So if I take that out,
my bond order increases.

00:14:40.745 --> 00:14:41.613
Right.

00:14:41.613 --> 00:14:42.747
OK.

00:14:42.747 --> 00:14:44.983
All right.

00:14:44.983 --> 00:14:47.552
And the last point I wanted
to make about MO theory

00:14:47.552 --> 00:14:49.354
before we talk
about hybridization

00:14:49.354 --> 00:14:52.190
is with one other
example, and that's HTL.

00:14:55.293 --> 00:14:56.494
OK.

00:14:56.494 --> 00:14:58.296
So for HTL, you
got something else

00:14:58.296 --> 00:14:59.865
happening that's important.

00:14:59.865 --> 00:15:03.401
So we just talked
about electronegativity

00:15:03.401 --> 00:15:06.872
driving where the-- you
know, that this is tilted.

00:15:06.872 --> 00:15:07.372
OK.

00:15:07.372 --> 00:15:08.273
Fine.

00:15:08.273 --> 00:15:10.842
And now we got the
fact that chlorine

00:15:10.842 --> 00:15:12.644
comes into this situation.

00:15:12.644 --> 00:15:17.749
You know, it's like chlorine is
like, hey, I got 7 electrons.

00:15:17.749 --> 00:15:22.520
And hydrogen is
like, hey, I got 1.

00:15:22.520 --> 00:15:24.522
So how's this work, right.

00:15:24.522 --> 00:15:27.525
So this gets to what was
brought up Friday, which

00:15:27.525 --> 00:15:31.062
is, there's another type of MO.

00:15:31.062 --> 00:15:33.531
And it's not bonding,
and it's not antibonding,

00:15:33.531 --> 00:15:35.433
it's non-bonding.

00:15:35.433 --> 00:15:37.235
That is not the
same as antibonding.

00:15:37.235 --> 00:15:40.772
Non-bonding doesn't
take sides, it just is.

00:15:40.772 --> 00:15:44.309
Because the way this works
is hydrogen comes at this--

00:15:44.309 --> 00:15:44.809
OK.

00:15:44.809 --> 00:15:48.713
So hydrogen's up
here with this 1s.

00:15:48.713 --> 00:15:51.549
Chlorine is, you know,
let's say over here.

00:15:51.549 --> 00:15:55.820
It's got 3s filled,
and it's got--

00:15:55.820 --> 00:15:58.623
let's draw these here.

00:15:58.623 --> 00:15:59.357
OK.

00:15:59.357 --> 00:16:01.092
3p.

00:16:01.092 --> 00:16:06.464
And chlorine's got 7
electrons in the 3p.

00:16:06.464 --> 00:16:10.168
Do they-- do they all come
together and form MOs?

00:16:10.168 --> 00:16:14.372
No, they can't, because there's
only 1 on this side, right.

00:16:14.372 --> 00:16:18.910
So this 1 orbital will form--

00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:23.281
let me make myself
enough space so I'm

00:16:23.281 --> 00:16:25.116
going to exaggerate
this a little bit.

00:16:25.116 --> 00:16:32.724
So this 1 orbital will
form a sigma bond.

00:16:32.724 --> 00:16:37.495
Sigma 3p, sigma 3p bond.

00:16:37.495 --> 00:16:39.965
This would be a sigma 3p star.

00:16:39.965 --> 00:16:45.203
Now, that's exactly what we've
already done, right, the sigma.

00:16:45.203 --> 00:16:51.576
But-- but-- oh, there's
something a little interesting

00:16:51.576 --> 00:16:54.980
about this one, right,
because it's an s.

00:16:54.980 --> 00:16:56.114
It's an s.

00:16:56.114 --> 00:17:00.685
But that's OK because the
s can talk to a sigma p.

00:17:00.685 --> 00:17:02.020
This is -- we talked about this.

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:04.255
The s can talk to this.

00:17:04.255 --> 00:17:05.790
That's OK.

00:17:05.790 --> 00:17:08.859
Because it's aligned, right.

00:17:08.859 --> 00:17:11.429
Now, but these guys don't
have anyone to talk to.

00:17:11.429 --> 00:17:11.896
They don't.

00:17:11.896 --> 00:17:15.032
So they can't make
their pi orbitals.

00:17:15.032 --> 00:17:16.434
They can't do that.

00:17:16.434 --> 00:17:18.336
And so they just stay there.

00:17:18.336 --> 00:17:22.173
These stay there exactly
at the same place.

00:17:22.173 --> 00:17:23.875
They're not doing
anything different.

00:17:23.875 --> 00:17:25.076
They're just hanging out.

00:17:25.076 --> 00:17:26.611
Those electrons are non-bonding.

00:17:26.611 --> 00:17:29.781
So these are not pi orbitals.

00:17:29.781 --> 00:17:33.051
Sigma and pi are special names.

00:17:33.051 --> 00:17:37.522
They refer to these orbitals
of the molecule that

00:17:37.522 --> 00:17:40.658
involve the LCAO, the
linear combination

00:17:40.658 --> 00:17:42.927
of the atomic orbitals.

00:17:42.927 --> 00:17:44.929
These do not.

00:17:44.929 --> 00:17:47.365
Nb.

00:17:47.365 --> 00:17:50.068
Nb.

00:17:50.068 --> 00:17:51.836
That's what we'll call them.

00:17:51.836 --> 00:17:53.671
Just to be very clear.

00:17:53.671 --> 00:17:55.040
Just to be very clear.

00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:56.574
Now, they're filled.

00:17:56.574 --> 00:18:00.378
They're filled, and they do
not count in the bond order.

00:18:00.378 --> 00:18:06.684
By the way, this is, also,
a non-bonding orbital.

00:18:06.684 --> 00:18:10.688
That's a non-bonding orbital.

00:18:10.688 --> 00:18:12.390
Non-bonding orbitals.

00:18:12.390 --> 00:18:13.925
These are not going to count.

00:18:13.925 --> 00:18:18.963
So in the bond order,
you only have--

00:18:18.963 --> 00:18:24.502
you have 1/2 times 2
minus 0 equals one.

00:18:24.502 --> 00:18:27.138
These do not
participate in bonding.

00:18:27.138 --> 00:18:28.673
I got to fill this.

00:18:28.673 --> 00:18:31.509
2 electrons are in that one.

00:18:31.509 --> 00:18:31.976
OK.

00:18:31.976 --> 00:18:34.512
So that's another
example of something

00:18:34.512 --> 00:18:37.482
that happens in
these MO diagrams.

00:18:37.482 --> 00:18:40.418
One of them brought too
many electrons to the party.

00:18:40.418 --> 00:18:43.321
And so there's no
one to talk to.

00:18:43.321 --> 00:18:43.888
But it's OK.

00:18:43.888 --> 00:18:45.690
They're just going to
stay there and wait.

00:18:45.690 --> 00:18:47.692
Maybe something else
will come along.

00:18:47.692 --> 00:18:48.426
Right.

00:18:48.426 --> 00:18:49.527
OK.

00:18:49.527 --> 00:18:53.231
Now, that is, right there,
what I just drew, look at that.

00:18:53.231 --> 00:18:56.701
2p 2s, and you get
this thing, right.

00:18:56.701 --> 00:18:59.270
Oh, it's a beautiful
molecular orbital between--

00:18:59.270 --> 00:18:59.804
OK.

00:18:59.804 --> 00:19:00.271
Fine.

00:19:00.271 --> 00:19:03.875
It was a 1s and a 3p,
but you get the point.

00:19:03.875 --> 00:19:07.378
It was a p oriented along
the axis with sigma symmetry.

00:19:07.378 --> 00:19:09.647
That means if I look
along the axis-- sorry,

00:19:09.647 --> 00:19:12.450
cylindrical
symmetry, sigma means

00:19:12.450 --> 00:19:14.119
if I look around the
axis, everything is

00:19:14.119 --> 00:19:16.654
the same, in a circle, right?

00:19:16.654 --> 00:19:17.288
Right.

00:19:17.288 --> 00:19:21.192
So-- but-- and so that's
a nice sigma orbital

00:19:21.192 --> 00:19:23.128
between two different atoms.

00:19:23.128 --> 00:19:23.828
MO theory.

00:19:23.828 --> 00:19:26.397
But see the other thing that
can happen that is something

00:19:26.397 --> 00:19:29.100
that I want you
to know today, is

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:34.205
that orbitals on the exact
same atom can also do this.

00:19:34.205 --> 00:19:36.474
They can also do this.

00:19:36.474 --> 00:19:42.113
They can combine to
form a new orbital.

00:19:42.113 --> 00:19:46.050
sp, same atom forms
an sp orbital.

00:19:46.050 --> 00:19:47.952
It's called a hybrid orbital.

00:19:47.952 --> 00:19:49.787
So we're going to give
a couple of examples.

00:19:49.787 --> 00:19:52.490
This is hybridization.

00:19:52.490 --> 00:19:53.558
Hybridization.

00:19:57.162 --> 00:19:59.297
And I can give a
couple of examples,

00:19:59.297 --> 00:20:01.199
then my [INAUDIBLE] matters.

00:20:01.199 --> 00:20:12.710
So this is when two or more
ao's that are similar in energy.

00:20:12.710 --> 00:20:15.213
They're similar, they're not
the same but they're close.

00:20:15.213 --> 00:20:17.415
You know, you can't
hybridize if one level's

00:20:17.415 --> 00:20:19.684
way up here and the other
level's way down there.

00:20:19.684 --> 00:20:26.191
But if they're close, 2s
2p, then those orbitals

00:20:26.191 --> 00:20:26.724
can combine.

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:31.996
And what they do is
they combine to form,

00:20:31.996 --> 00:20:47.078
and this is critical, sets of
equivalent orbitals properly

00:20:47.078 --> 00:20:48.246
oriented.

00:20:48.246 --> 00:20:49.280
This is really important.

00:20:49.280 --> 00:20:50.648
That's why I'm
writing it all out.

00:20:50.648 --> 00:20:52.917
Properly, not property.

00:20:52.917 --> 00:20:58.089
Properly oriented to form bonds.

00:20:58.089 --> 00:21:04.128
Oh, this is such a big
deal, to form bonds.

00:21:04.128 --> 00:21:07.665
It's the last word there, but
I did it all caps to emphasize.

00:21:07.665 --> 00:21:09.867
Because that's what
this is all about.

00:21:09.867 --> 00:21:13.271
You see, it-- now,
let's take an example.

00:21:13.271 --> 00:21:14.939
I'm going to take an
example of methane.

00:21:19.644 --> 00:21:20.945
Who brings what to the party?

00:21:20.945 --> 00:21:21.579
OK.

00:21:21.579 --> 00:21:22.747
I got H--

00:21:22.747 --> 00:21:24.315
I've got 4 H's.

00:21:24.315 --> 00:21:31.889
H1s, H1s, H1s, OK.

00:21:31.889 --> 00:21:36.427
This is definitely
getting repetitive.

00:21:36.427 --> 00:21:36.894
H1s.

00:21:39.697 --> 00:21:40.765
But I did it.

00:21:40.765 --> 00:21:41.666
There they are.

00:21:41.666 --> 00:21:44.335
And they come, and they're
like, hey, carbon, what you got?

00:21:44.335 --> 00:21:46.604
And the carbon is
like, OK, hold up.

00:21:46.604 --> 00:21:51.142
I got my 2s, that's
here, and I got my--

00:21:51.142 --> 00:21:56.981
all my 2p's, and
what are we to do?

00:21:56.981 --> 00:21:58.716
What are we to do?

00:21:58.716 --> 00:22:01.619
How can these four
hydrogens come

00:22:01.619 --> 00:22:05.423
and bond with the carbon
given that it looks,

00:22:05.423 --> 00:22:10.528
at least, like the carbon isn't
bringing the kind-- you know,

00:22:10.528 --> 00:22:11.629
how's it going to do this?

00:22:11.629 --> 00:22:13.097
And, also-- and
this is critical.

00:22:13.097 --> 00:22:14.932
This is why I wrote it here.

00:22:14.932 --> 00:22:17.468
Properly oriented to form bonds.

00:22:17.468 --> 00:22:20.371
You see, because this
taps into what we

00:22:20.371 --> 00:22:24.309
learned with VSEPR It
does, because VSEPR

00:22:24.309 --> 00:22:27.211
says you got to not repel.

00:22:27.211 --> 00:22:30.214
Or, no, you're repelling,
but try to minimize it.

00:22:30.214 --> 00:22:33.117
I mean, that's what's
at the core of VSEPR.

00:22:33.117 --> 00:22:37.121
So what this system
sees is a way out.

00:22:37.121 --> 00:22:39.657
And by way out, I mean
a way to happiness.

00:22:39.657 --> 00:22:41.492
And by a way to
happiness, I mean a way

00:22:41.492 --> 00:22:45.363
to lower energy where all 4 of
these can bond to the carbon

00:22:45.363 --> 00:22:49.467
atom if it has 4 same bonds.

00:22:49.467 --> 00:22:54.872
If it had 4 same bonds, then it
can orient them tetrahedrally

00:22:54.872 --> 00:23:00.311
and make VSEPR happy,
minimize repulsions, right.

00:23:00.311 --> 00:23:02.513
And you get your
methane molecule.

00:23:02.513 --> 00:23:07.251
So what ends up happening
is this carbon atoms says,

00:23:07.251 --> 00:23:08.519
hold up.

00:23:08.519 --> 00:23:10.688
You 4 hydrogens,
you wait there, I'm

00:23:10.688 --> 00:23:15.593
just going to go into the back
and do a little reorganization,

00:23:15.593 --> 00:23:17.895
and I'm going to
come in it with--

00:23:17.895 --> 00:23:19.530
I'm going to say,
well, if I had--

00:23:19.530 --> 00:23:23.067
if my carbon atom comes at--

00:23:23.067 --> 00:23:33.511
comes at it with 4 equal
orbitals that look like this,

00:23:33.511 --> 00:23:39.016
those are called sp3, because
I took all 3 p orbitals

00:23:39.016 --> 00:23:41.085
and I mix them up
with my s orbital

00:23:41.085 --> 00:23:46.190
and I formed
equivalent orbitals.

00:23:46.190 --> 00:23:50.027
Now, carbon comes out of the
back room, and it's like,

00:23:50.027 --> 00:23:54.465
hydrogens, I'm ready, I
can make a minimum energy,

00:23:54.465 --> 00:23:55.900
and I can follow
VSEPR and we can

00:23:55.900 --> 00:23:59.203
have-- all have very,
very low energy, compared

00:23:59.203 --> 00:24:01.839
to whatever else I could've
done, if I stuck like that.

00:24:04.642 --> 00:24:06.644
What I'm really talking
about when I'm talk--

00:24:06.644 --> 00:24:07.211
OK.

00:24:07.211 --> 00:24:09.046
You might know
carbon, you know, it

00:24:09.046 --> 00:24:10.948
doesn't go into the back room.

00:24:10.948 --> 00:24:11.449
It doesn't.

00:24:11.449 --> 00:24:12.917
But what is it doing?

00:24:12.917 --> 00:24:15.486
It's nothing more than solving
its Schrodinger equation.

00:24:15.486 --> 00:24:17.488
That's what this is all about.

00:24:17.488 --> 00:24:20.591
You just change the
boundary conditions.

00:24:20.591 --> 00:24:23.694
And so what happened--
that's all hybridization is.

00:24:23.694 --> 00:24:26.697
You're solving the Schrodinger
equation all the time.

00:24:26.697 --> 00:24:29.600
We are always solving
the Schrodinger equation

00:24:29.600 --> 00:24:30.968
all the time.

00:24:30.968 --> 00:24:32.503
And in this case,
carbon said, well,

00:24:32.503 --> 00:24:36.140
how can I solve it and minimize
my energy with the 4 hydrogens?

00:24:36.140 --> 00:24:38.342
This gives you
the lowest energy.

00:24:38.342 --> 00:24:42.513
Because, now--
oh, I got a movie.

00:24:42.513 --> 00:24:45.216
Now, I can make those, see.

00:24:45.216 --> 00:24:46.150
So I have them--

00:24:46.150 --> 00:24:47.251
I have a picture here.

00:24:47.251 --> 00:24:49.353
s and those three.

00:24:49.353 --> 00:24:51.689
And it says, how can I do
it and minimize repulsions?

00:24:51.689 --> 00:24:52.723
This is how.

00:24:52.723 --> 00:24:55.526
Three equivalent-- 4 equivalent
orbitals to make it sp3.

00:24:55.526 --> 00:24:57.161
And this is where I
think a movie helps.

00:24:57.161 --> 00:24:57.895
So I'm going to play this.

00:24:57.895 --> 00:24:58.896
It's like a minute long.

00:24:58.896 --> 00:25:01.732
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:25:01.732 --> 00:25:04.702
Each 2s orbital is
a two lobe shape

00:25:04.702 --> 00:25:06.471
converging at the nucleus.

00:25:06.471 --> 00:25:07.371
- There's the 2s.

00:25:07.371 --> 00:25:10.475
So there are the
three 2p orbitals.

00:25:10.475 --> 00:25:12.977
However, when
hybridization occurs,

00:25:12.977 --> 00:25:16.080
the s and p orbitals
cease to exist,

00:25:16.113 --> 00:25:19.884
They don't cease to
exist, they just didn't--

00:25:19.884 --> 00:25:21.285
it's a new boundary condition.

00:25:21.285 --> 00:25:22.520
You're solving the equation.

00:25:22.520 --> 00:25:23.020
Right.

00:25:23.020 --> 00:25:24.755
So just to keep that
straight, right?

00:25:24.755 --> 00:25:25.490
These are the orbitals--

00:25:25.556 --> 00:25:29.193
and the two sp3 orbitals have
an entirely different shape.

00:25:29.193 --> 00:25:30.661
- That's true.

00:25:30.661 --> 00:25:33.030
We can see that orbital
hybridization explains

00:25:33.030 --> 00:25:37.134
the VSEPR placement of
carbons for VLANs electrons.

00:25:37.134 --> 00:25:40.638
Since all four 2sp3
orbitals are equivalent,

00:25:40.638 --> 00:25:44.675
each 2sp3 orbital repels
the others with equal force,

00:25:44.675 --> 00:25:47.144
resulting in
identical bond angles.

00:25:49.780 --> 00:25:52.116
The carbon atom
only hybridizes when

00:25:52.116 --> 00:25:54.151
it is in a bonding situation.

00:25:54.151 --> 00:25:56.854
Here, four hydrogen
atoms bond to carbon

00:25:56.854 --> 00:26:01.759
by overlapping their orbitals
with carbon's hybrid orbitals.

00:26:01.759 --> 00:26:04.161
So what would be
the reason for this?

00:26:04.161 --> 00:26:07.698
If we go back and see that
both carbon and hydrogen have

00:26:07.698 --> 00:26:10.868
unpaired electrons,
the overlap allows

00:26:10.868 --> 00:26:14.071
the electrons to pair, and thus
go to a lower potential energy.

00:26:14.071 --> 00:26:14.705
[END PLAYBACK]

00:26:14.705 --> 00:26:16.774
Oh, that's such a
beautiful place to stop.

00:26:16.774 --> 00:26:18.409
Lower potential energy.

00:26:18.409 --> 00:26:20.344
Happier.

00:26:20.344 --> 00:26:22.446
That's what this is all about.

00:26:22.446 --> 00:26:22.947
OK.

00:26:22.947 --> 00:26:25.816
So I-- you know, like I said,
you can see now the difference,

00:26:25.816 --> 00:26:27.952
right, if you just watch--
it's one minute long,

00:26:27.952 --> 00:26:29.620
and I think it really
brings it to life.

00:26:29.620 --> 00:26:32.056
So if you're, again, want
to see this a little bit

00:26:32.056 --> 00:26:33.724
more conceptually,
these movies are good.

00:26:33.724 --> 00:26:37.695
I have one more movie I'll show
you that's like a minute long.

00:26:37.695 --> 00:26:38.195
OK.

00:26:38.195 --> 00:26:39.897
Now, the thing is--
so what we just

00:26:39.897 --> 00:26:42.833
did is we did carbon
hybridizes to sp3.

00:26:42.833 --> 00:26:45.202
It's called sp3 because
you're bringing 3p orbitals

00:26:45.202 --> 00:26:47.838
and mixing them
with s to form 4.

00:26:47.838 --> 00:26:50.274
And that allowed it to do this.

00:26:50.274 --> 00:26:54.011
But, you see, if I had
ethane, which is C2H6,

00:26:54.011 --> 00:26:55.446
it's the same thing.

00:26:55.446 --> 00:26:58.215
Now-- because, basically,
what's happening?

00:26:58.215 --> 00:27:02.086
A carbon atom comes into this
system of ethane, and it says,

00:27:02.086 --> 00:27:06.390
I need 4 bonds that are
kind of equivalent-ish.

00:27:06.390 --> 00:27:06.991
All right.

00:27:06.991 --> 00:27:08.659
I got to give one
to each hydrogen

00:27:08.659 --> 00:27:12.229
and I've got to give one to
that other carbon over there.

00:27:12.229 --> 00:27:15.499
And by doing that, it
can minimize its energy,

00:27:15.499 --> 00:27:18.302
it can minimize repulsions, it
can be the happiest molecule

00:27:18.302 --> 00:27:19.303
it can be.

00:27:19.303 --> 00:27:24.308
But to do that, carbon must
have these four equivalent

00:27:24.308 --> 00:27:25.743
sigma like bonds.

00:27:25.743 --> 00:27:28.112
It must be able to form
those four equivalency.

00:27:28.112 --> 00:27:31.248
So that's very similar to
methane, except that in this--

00:27:31.248 --> 00:27:35.052
so, also, ethane will
be sp3 hybridized.

00:27:35.052 --> 00:27:38.656
It will have sp3
hybridized orbitals.

00:27:38.656 --> 00:27:40.791
But then, and you know
it's coming, right,

00:27:40.791 --> 00:27:44.161
so ethane has C2H6.

00:27:44.161 --> 00:27:48.165
But what if now
I've got ethylene.

00:27:48.165 --> 00:27:50.134
I think I have-- maybe
I have a picture of it.

00:27:50.134 --> 00:27:50.901
Do I have a picture of it?

00:27:50.901 --> 00:27:51.435
Yeah.

00:27:51.435 --> 00:27:52.536
Oh, there it is.

00:27:52.536 --> 00:27:53.337
Ethylene.

00:27:53.337 --> 00:27:54.405
Ethylene is used.

00:27:54.405 --> 00:27:58.542
This tiny molecule is 2 carbons
and 4 hydrogen instead of 6.

00:27:58.542 --> 00:28:00.778
This is used in so many
applications I can't possibly

00:28:00.778 --> 00:28:01.278
list them.

00:28:01.278 --> 00:28:04.715
If polyethylene is a polymer
made out of this as a base,

00:28:04.715 --> 00:28:07.718
we will polymerize this a
little bit later in the semester

00:28:07.718 --> 00:28:09.420
when we talk about polymers.

00:28:09.420 --> 00:28:11.922
It's also used in the back
of your supermarket in case

00:28:11.922 --> 00:28:12.823
you didn't know that.

00:28:12.823 --> 00:28:15.559
Very soon-- actually,
kind of almost now-ish,

00:28:15.559 --> 00:28:18.663
most of the fruit we get will be
super green when it gets here.

00:28:18.663 --> 00:28:24.301
And then they use ethylene
to actually induce, sadly,

00:28:24.301 --> 00:28:25.469
the brightness.

00:28:25.469 --> 00:28:27.571
Doesn't taste as good.

00:28:27.571 --> 00:28:28.472
But, anyway-- OK.

00:28:28.472 --> 00:28:32.476
Now, but for ethylene, you've
got a different situation.

00:28:32.476 --> 00:28:36.914
Because for ethylene, each
carbon atom needs 3 bonds.

00:28:36.914 --> 00:28:44.722
Each carbon atom needs
3 equivalent bonds.

00:28:48.859 --> 00:28:52.863
And it can do that, too.

00:28:52.863 --> 00:28:58.969
So, for C2H4, each carbon
atom needs 3 equivalent bonds,

00:28:58.969 --> 00:29:01.405
and it can do that
if it hybridizes

00:29:01.405 --> 00:29:03.040
in the sp2 hybridization.

00:29:03.040 --> 00:29:04.141
So let's draw that, right.

00:29:04.141 --> 00:29:08.746
So these are the original
orbitals of carbon.

00:29:08.746 --> 00:29:14.318
And they will go
to 3 sp2 orbitals.

00:29:16.854 --> 00:29:20.791
Notice, I can put one electron
in each ready for action.

00:29:20.791 --> 00:29:22.259
Ready for bonding.

00:29:22.259 --> 00:29:25.696
One electron in each of these
equivalent hybrid orbitals,

00:29:25.696 --> 00:29:26.530
sp2.

00:29:26.530 --> 00:29:28.165
And I got one left over.

00:29:28.165 --> 00:29:30.634
Ho, ho, oh.

00:29:30.634 --> 00:29:33.070
One left over.

00:29:33.070 --> 00:29:36.073
But the one left
over is a p orbital

00:29:36.073 --> 00:29:37.908
that didn't get in
on the hybridization.

00:29:37.908 --> 00:29:40.010
But that's OK.

00:29:40.010 --> 00:29:40.811
That's OK.

00:29:40.811 --> 00:29:42.446
These are p, right.

00:29:42.446 --> 00:29:46.183
So this is s and this is p2s 2p.

00:29:46.183 --> 00:29:47.651
And that's a p
orbital right there.

00:29:47.651 --> 00:29:49.553
Those are my hybrids,
and that's a 2p orbital.

00:29:52.256 --> 00:29:54.892
There's another 2p orbital
on the other carbon.

00:29:54.892 --> 00:29:56.260
Also, got left out.

00:29:56.260 --> 00:29:58.095
Now, I only needed
3 because, you

00:29:58.095 --> 00:30:02.466
see, I formed this
nice spatially maximize

00:30:02.466 --> 00:30:04.835
to minimize repulsion,
right, separation,

00:30:04.835 --> 00:30:07.304
maximize to minimize
repulsion so it's a planar--

00:30:07.304 --> 00:30:10.040
oh, but it's a planar
for another reason.

00:30:10.040 --> 00:30:13.110
Because each of these carbon
atoms has a p orbital,

00:30:13.110 --> 00:30:18.682
and we know that 2p orbitals
can form a pi bond, right?

00:30:18.682 --> 00:30:20.117
And I have a picture of that.

00:30:20.117 --> 00:30:22.219
That's exactly what they do.

00:30:22.219 --> 00:30:26.023
So this is the sigma.

00:30:26.023 --> 00:30:28.592
The hybrid orbitals
are sigma, right,

00:30:28.592 --> 00:30:31.495
because they're along the
axis, and look at them.

00:30:31.495 --> 00:30:35.800
If I look down this axis,
I can draw a circle,

00:30:35.800 --> 00:30:38.936
and there's full
cylindrical symmetry, right.

00:30:38.936 --> 00:30:41.705
But I've got this
left over p orbital

00:30:41.705 --> 00:30:44.208
here on each carbon atom.

00:30:44.208 --> 00:30:46.310
That's that one there.

00:30:46.310 --> 00:30:49.580
And I didn't need it to do
this but it's still there.

00:30:49.580 --> 00:30:51.148
And so it forms a bond.

00:30:51.148 --> 00:30:53.150
It forms a bond.

00:30:53.150 --> 00:30:53.684
Right.

00:30:53.684 --> 00:30:55.986
It forms a pi bond.

00:30:55.986 --> 00:30:59.623
So that p with the p
from the other one forms

00:30:59.623 --> 00:31:02.893
a pi and a pi star,
right, in the MOs,

00:31:02.893 --> 00:31:05.696
and you occupy the pi
with the two electrons.

00:31:05.696 --> 00:31:08.432
And that's a bond.

00:31:08.432 --> 00:31:10.100
So in this you've got--

00:31:10.100 --> 00:31:14.338
you've got a bond from this
that's above or below--

00:31:14.338 --> 00:31:18.008
in this orientation, it's
above or below the plane.

00:31:18.008 --> 00:31:19.643
And you've got a bond from this.

00:31:19.643 --> 00:31:21.178
So you've got a double bond.

00:31:21.178 --> 00:31:24.281
That's exactly what
we know already,

00:31:24.281 --> 00:31:26.984
but we know so much more now.

00:31:26.984 --> 00:31:28.018
We know so much more.

00:31:28.018 --> 00:31:32.022
Because-- because, now, we
know that it's not just two--

00:31:32.022 --> 00:31:33.858
you know, two bonds
and four electrons,

00:31:33.858 --> 00:31:35.492
we know that they
have shape to them.

00:31:35.492 --> 00:31:37.194
We know that they have
structure to them,

00:31:37.194 --> 00:31:40.197
and we know that those
bonds are different.

00:31:40.197 --> 00:31:43.667
These are pi bonds, and
that's a sigma bond.

00:31:43.667 --> 00:31:44.568
Right.

00:31:44.568 --> 00:31:47.838
And you, also, know why
this molecule stays planar.

00:31:47.838 --> 00:31:51.408
Because if I try to twist
it, then those p orbitals

00:31:51.408 --> 00:31:53.143
won't align, right.

00:31:53.143 --> 00:31:54.211
It's like a little spring.

00:31:54.211 --> 00:31:56.580
If I-- if I try to twist it,
it's going to go right back.

00:31:56.580 --> 00:31:59.283
Because it's minimum in
energy is when these things

00:31:59.283 --> 00:32:01.051
can maximize their overlap.

00:32:01.051 --> 00:32:01.518
All right.

00:32:01.518 --> 00:32:03.787
So that's going to stay planar.

00:32:03.787 --> 00:32:06.023
OK.

00:32:06.023 --> 00:32:12.529
Now-- and so for this case,
you've got a double bond.

00:32:12.529 --> 00:32:21.805
So this is double bond, and I
think I have a little carbon,

00:32:21.805 --> 00:32:24.875
carbon, double bond.

00:32:24.875 --> 00:32:26.777
And I think I've got
a video to show this,

00:32:26.777 --> 00:32:28.946
because this is another
case where, you know,

00:32:28.946 --> 00:32:30.781
again, I just want to
make sure you guys get

00:32:30.781 --> 00:32:32.449
the intuition here,
and I thought this is

00:32:32.449 --> 00:32:34.451
a pretty cool animation of sp2.

00:32:34.451 --> 00:32:35.719
So I'll play it for a minute.

00:32:35.719 --> 00:32:36.420
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:32:36.420 --> 00:32:37.521
[INAUDIBLE] bonds.

00:32:37.521 --> 00:32:41.992
The one 2s and two of the
2p orbitals hybridize.

00:32:41.992 --> 00:32:42.893
Consequently--

00:32:42.893 --> 00:32:43.894
- That's cool animation.

00:32:43.894 --> 00:32:46.697
--this hybridization is
termed as sp2 hybridization.

00:32:46.697 --> 00:32:48.365
- Look at that.

00:32:48.365 --> 00:32:52.569
The hybridization leads to
the formation of 3 [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:52.569 --> 00:32:53.904
sp2 hybrid orbitals.

00:32:57.007 --> 00:33:01.712
As you can see, each sp2
hybrid orbital is bilobed.

00:33:01.712 --> 00:33:03.914
One lobe bigger than the other.

00:33:03.914 --> 00:33:06.550
The half-filled p
orbital, which was not

00:33:06.550 --> 00:33:10.120
involved in hybridization,
flies at right angles

00:33:10.120 --> 00:33:14.024
to the plane of the
equilateral triangle.

00:33:14.024 --> 00:33:16.560
Now, let us understand
how this hybridized

00:33:16.560 --> 00:33:21.031
state results in the
formation of a double bond.

00:33:21.031 --> 00:33:26.136
For this, imagine a similar
sp2 hybridized carbon atom

00:33:26.136 --> 00:33:28.038
approaching this carbon atom.

00:33:28.038 --> 00:33:31.608
As these atoms come
closer, an orbital overlap

00:33:31.608 --> 00:33:35.512
takes place along the
internuclear axes.

00:33:35.512 --> 00:33:38.615
This bond is called
a sigma bond.

00:33:38.615 --> 00:33:41.518
At this stage, the
unhybridized p orbitals

00:33:41.518 --> 00:33:45.689
which lie above and below the
plane of the sigma bond, also

00:33:45.689 --> 00:33:49.059
come very close to each
other and overlap laterally,

00:33:49.059 --> 00:33:52.863
resulting in the formation of a
pi bond between the two carbon

00:33:52.863 --> 00:33:53.330
atoms.

00:33:53.330 --> 00:33:53.764
[END PLAYBACK]

00:33:53.764 --> 00:33:54.264
All right.

00:33:54.264 --> 00:33:54.765
OK.

00:33:54.765 --> 00:33:58.435
So I thought about it.

00:33:58.435 --> 00:34:01.638
I watched that movie, of course,
many times over the weekend.

00:34:01.638 --> 00:34:04.408
And I thought about can
I animate, you know,

00:34:04.408 --> 00:34:07.044
the electrons like that
and do the-- and I--

00:34:07.044 --> 00:34:08.012
I think I could.

00:34:08.012 --> 00:34:10.813
But I don't know that it would
be a good use of our time.

00:34:10.813 --> 00:34:13.784
So I thought that they did a
pretty good job of bringing it

00:34:13.784 --> 00:34:14.918
to life.

00:34:14.918 --> 00:34:18.722
Now, this is a great
time for me to tell you

00:34:18.722 --> 00:34:20.824
about my why this matters.

00:34:20.824 --> 00:34:23.159
And, of course,
the sp2 carbon atom

00:34:23.159 --> 00:34:29.733
matters because of
drinking water, of course.

00:34:29.733 --> 00:34:31.368
And, you know, this is a well--

00:34:31.368 --> 00:34:34.938
in a district in India where
more than 50% of the wells

00:34:34.938 --> 00:34:37.306
exceed the WHO
limits for arsenic

00:34:37.306 --> 00:34:41.277
by around a factor of 5.

00:34:41.277 --> 00:34:44.380
1.8 billion people in the world
drink fecally contaminated

00:34:44.380 --> 00:34:46.016
water on a daily basis.

00:34:46.016 --> 00:34:49.953
600 million people boil
their water in the world.

00:34:49.953 --> 00:34:51.755
Boiling doesn't help
with arsenic, though,

00:34:51.755 --> 00:34:54.123
arsenic just stays
in the boiled water.

00:34:54.123 --> 00:34:57.327
It kills bacteria, but it
doesn't help with toxic--

00:34:57.327 --> 00:34:59.830
toxic elements.

00:34:59.830 --> 00:35:01.965
If you look at the
world as a whole,

00:35:01.965 --> 00:35:06.136
and you look at sort of
where there are water--

00:35:06.136 --> 00:35:10.040
water crisis, where the water
crisis is at this level,

00:35:10.040 --> 00:35:14.578
it's a little over 3
and 1/2 billion people.

00:35:14.578 --> 00:35:19.716
And in those countries,
almost 185 or so countries,

00:35:19.716 --> 00:35:21.985
where this is a
serious problem, if you

00:35:21.985 --> 00:35:26.723
look at the cost of disease,
and the cause of death

00:35:26.723 --> 00:35:30.994
in those countries, 70% to 80%
of all disease and almost 30%

00:35:30.994 --> 00:35:34.131
of all death can be attributed
to the water quality.

00:35:34.131 --> 00:35:34.798
Right.

00:35:34.798 --> 00:35:38.001
So when I talk about
water as a problem,

00:35:38.001 --> 00:35:40.604
I really mean it's
a serious problem.

00:35:40.604 --> 00:35:43.540
It's a problem of life or death.

00:35:43.540 --> 00:35:47.077
Now, this is-- to get
fresh drinking water,

00:35:47.077 --> 00:35:49.646
if you look at the planet,
and you say, well, where

00:35:49.646 --> 00:35:51.381
is the water on this planet?

00:35:51.381 --> 00:35:54.017
Where is the water
on this planet?

00:35:54.017 --> 00:36:01.158
I like this picture because we
consume water volumetrically,

00:36:01.158 --> 00:36:02.759
not in an area, right?

00:36:02.759 --> 00:36:05.362
We use water as a
volume, and this

00:36:05.362 --> 00:36:10.200
is the water we have on this
precious planet as a volume.

00:36:10.200 --> 00:36:14.004
This bubble here is
all of the ocean water.

00:36:14.004 --> 00:36:17.141
It's about 70-- it's about
97% of all the water.

00:36:17.141 --> 00:36:21.578
That bubble there is freshwater
that is inaccessible.

00:36:21.578 --> 00:36:22.412
It's frozen.

00:36:22.412 --> 00:36:24.214
And this one here, you
can't really see it,

00:36:24.214 --> 00:36:25.949
there is another dot there.

00:36:25.949 --> 00:36:28.218
That's less than 1%
of the world's water.

00:36:28.218 --> 00:36:30.654
That is our drinking
water ecosystem

00:36:30.654 --> 00:36:33.957
that is what we are destroying.

00:36:33.957 --> 00:36:35.192
And-- so what--

00:36:35.192 --> 00:36:35.692
OK.

00:36:35.692 --> 00:36:39.930
So there's a lot of things
we can try to do about this.

00:36:39.930 --> 00:36:41.331
One of the things
we can try to do

00:36:41.331 --> 00:36:43.867
is to see if we
can tap into this

00:36:43.867 --> 00:36:47.070
in a way that's more
affordable and more efficient.

00:36:47.070 --> 00:36:51.408
Because there's a lot of water
here, right, and use that.

00:36:51.408 --> 00:36:54.444
But the problem is, if you look
at the cost of desalination

00:36:54.444 --> 00:36:57.681
today as opposed to the
cost-- that's desalination.

00:36:57.681 --> 00:36:59.082
If you look at the
cost as opposed

00:36:59.082 --> 00:37:01.251
to just digging
water out of a well,

00:37:01.251 --> 00:37:02.886
like the one I just
showed you in India,

00:37:02.886 --> 00:37:04.955
it's over a factor of 10.

00:37:04.955 --> 00:37:05.722
Still.

00:37:05.722 --> 00:37:08.992
And, in fact, one of the
big issues with desalination

00:37:08.992 --> 00:37:12.462
isn't just the total cost
or the operating costs,

00:37:12.462 --> 00:37:13.530
but the capital cost.

00:37:13.530 --> 00:37:17.734
How do you build this
plant in the first place?

00:37:17.734 --> 00:37:22.539
And so it takes too much money.

00:37:22.539 --> 00:37:26.210
If you look at the thing that's
at the heart of desalination,

00:37:26.210 --> 00:37:28.011
it's a filter, right.

00:37:28.011 --> 00:37:28.612
It's a filter.

00:37:28.612 --> 00:37:30.013
And I talked about
this in my last,

00:37:30.013 --> 00:37:32.849
why this matters, when I
talk about separations,

00:37:32.849 --> 00:37:35.018
and I promised that in my
next why does this matter,

00:37:35.018 --> 00:37:36.553
I'd talk about water.

00:37:36.553 --> 00:37:37.120
Right.

00:37:37.120 --> 00:37:38.755
Which is what I'm doing.

00:37:38.755 --> 00:37:40.190
If you look at a
desal plant, this

00:37:40.190 --> 00:37:42.392
is one of the world's largest,
this is a plant called

00:37:42.392 --> 00:37:44.494
the Hadera plant
in Israel, and you

00:37:44.494 --> 00:37:46.396
look at what their costs
are that-- remember,

00:37:46.396 --> 00:37:48.999
the costs digits-- half of that
cost-- almost half of the cost

00:37:48.999 --> 00:37:50.667
isn't just energy.

00:37:50.667 --> 00:37:54.104
And almost all of that energy
is in pushing salt water

00:37:54.104 --> 00:37:55.672
through a filter.

00:37:55.672 --> 00:37:57.774
Through a membrane.

00:37:57.774 --> 00:37:59.676
That's called a reverse
osmosis membrane,

00:37:59.676 --> 00:38:03.647
because you're going against
the osmotic pressure.

00:38:03.647 --> 00:38:06.216
And if you look at
the membrane itself--

00:38:06.216 --> 00:38:07.784
this is a picture
of it, it's actually

00:38:07.784 --> 00:38:11.588
a very small layer on top
of this active layer that

00:38:11.588 --> 00:38:13.023
does all the work--

00:38:13.023 --> 00:38:15.325
it's not a very good design.

00:38:15.325 --> 00:38:19.963
In fact, membranes for
desalination are pretty bad.

00:38:19.963 --> 00:38:21.164
Right.

00:38:21.164 --> 00:38:23.800
They kind of do everything
worse than they should,

00:38:23.800 --> 00:38:27.170
except that they work, which
is good, and they're cheap,

00:38:27.170 --> 00:38:28.405
$1.00 a square foot.

00:38:28.405 --> 00:38:30.607
But, see, they're very--
they take much more energy

00:38:30.607 --> 00:38:32.542
than you need.

00:38:32.542 --> 00:38:34.611
They foul up very easily.

00:38:34.611 --> 00:38:37.047
And then that means stuff
gets kind of, you know,

00:38:37.047 --> 00:38:38.548
stuck in them.

00:38:38.548 --> 00:38:41.985
And then you can't take
it-- you can't clean them

00:38:41.985 --> 00:38:44.621
because these polyamide
membranes, which

00:38:44.621 --> 00:38:47.357
are the same polymer used in
these membranes for 50 years,

00:38:47.357 --> 00:38:50.460
haven't changed, the material.

00:38:50.460 --> 00:38:52.929
Those polyamide membranes
are destroyed by chlorine.

00:38:52.929 --> 00:38:56.166
So even in a desal
plant-- in a desal plant,

00:38:56.166 --> 00:38:59.936
if you have drinking
water in your feed stream,

00:38:59.936 --> 00:39:02.205
drinking water has six
parts per million chlorine.

00:39:02.205 --> 00:39:03.373
That's not much.

00:39:03.373 --> 00:39:07.177
They still will go through
the cost to remove it.

00:39:07.177 --> 00:39:08.578
They will remove
it from the feed.

00:39:08.578 --> 00:39:09.079
Why?

00:39:09.079 --> 00:39:11.715
Because if you leave that little
amount of chlorine in the feed,

00:39:11.715 --> 00:39:12.949
