00:40:41.571 --> 00:40:46.576
This beautiful material that
we have now understood more

00:40:46.576 --> 00:40:47.544
than we did before.

00:40:47.544 --> 00:40:49.479
Because, remember,
before I showed

00:40:49.479 --> 00:40:51.114
you graphene and I showed you--

00:40:51.114 --> 00:40:52.115
no, I showed you benzene.

00:40:54.651 --> 00:40:57.220
And we talked about it as
a Lewis resonant structure,

00:40:57.220 --> 00:40:57.921
remember?

00:40:57.921 --> 00:40:58.422
Right.

00:40:58.422 --> 00:41:01.658
A Lewis resonant structure to
help explain how it looked.

00:41:01.658 --> 00:41:06.062
You know, gra-- graphene does
not have alternating bonds.

00:41:06.062 --> 00:41:08.231
Remember, that's what
we talked about before.

00:41:08.231 --> 00:41:11.034
It's a Lewis resonant structure.

00:41:11.034 --> 00:41:13.136
So it lowers its
energy by having

00:41:13.136 --> 00:41:17.574
two sets of structures that
have sort of alternating bonds.

00:41:17.574 --> 00:41:19.576
We talked about it in
the context of benzene.

00:41:19.576 --> 00:41:22.612
But, see, now you
know so much more.

00:41:22.612 --> 00:41:23.847
You know so much more.

00:41:23.847 --> 00:41:27.884
Because, now, you know why
graphene is so special.

00:41:27.884 --> 00:41:29.886
You know why graphene
is so special.

00:41:29.886 --> 00:41:33.156
It actually is
the hybridization.

00:41:33.156 --> 00:41:34.157
It's the hybridization.

00:41:34.157 --> 00:41:36.893
Because on top,
and on the bottom,

00:41:36.893 --> 00:41:41.331
of a single atom of a
single sheet of graphene,

00:41:41.331 --> 00:41:44.367
you've got pi
bonding all the way.

00:41:44.367 --> 00:41:47.370
Those pi bands are going
across the whole surface.

00:41:47.370 --> 00:41:49.706
And those electrons
are critical.

00:41:49.706 --> 00:41:51.641
Those electrons that
occupy those [INAUDIBLE]

00:41:51.641 --> 00:41:57.647
are critical to the very special
properties that graphene has.

00:41:57.647 --> 00:42:00.016
So you now know the secret.

00:42:00.016 --> 00:42:01.751
It's sp2 hybridization.

00:42:01.751 --> 00:42:04.287
And, you know, this
has allowed us--

00:42:04.287 --> 00:42:06.490
this has-- you might
tell I'm kind of

00:42:06.490 --> 00:42:07.824
passionate about this problem.

00:42:07.824 --> 00:42:09.359
Partly impassioned passion
about a lot of problems

00:42:09.359 --> 00:42:10.927
but, also, we happen
to work on this,

00:42:10.927 --> 00:42:12.629
and we have been
developing this.

00:42:12.629 --> 00:42:14.731
This is like the
ultimate membrane.

00:42:14.731 --> 00:42:16.833
I can soak this in
chlorine overnight.

00:42:16.833 --> 00:42:18.668
I can put it in
negative pH solutions.

00:42:18.668 --> 00:42:20.103
I can heat it up.

00:42:20.103 --> 00:42:22.038
It doesn't degrade.

00:42:22.038 --> 00:42:26.042
And we have figured out
how to poke holes in it

00:42:26.042 --> 00:42:28.211
at just the right
size, or to stitch it

00:42:28.211 --> 00:42:33.216
together so that maybe you can
filter particles by the flow

00:42:33.216 --> 00:42:34.951
in between these sheets.

00:42:34.951 --> 00:42:36.686
Right.

00:42:36.686 --> 00:42:38.288
And this material,
as a membrane,

00:42:38.288 --> 00:42:41.525
is so promising
that we've actually

00:42:41.525 --> 00:42:44.227
started to commercialize
it as of the last year.

00:42:44.227 --> 00:42:46.596
And I think this is really
going to make a big difference

00:42:46.596 --> 00:42:48.798
in a lot of areas.

00:42:48.798 --> 00:42:49.599
Why?

00:42:49.599 --> 00:42:52.135
Because of sp2 hybridization.

00:42:52.135 --> 00:42:53.236
That is why.

00:42:53.236 --> 00:42:55.772
That is why this is
such a special material.

00:42:55.772 --> 00:42:56.339
OK.

00:42:56.339 --> 00:42:58.441
That's my why this matters.

00:42:58.441 --> 00:43:00.610
Now-- oh, we did see 2H6.

00:43:00.610 --> 00:43:01.144
There it is.

00:43:01.144 --> 00:43:02.345
We did see 2H4.

00:43:02.345 --> 00:43:03.046
There it is.

00:43:03.046 --> 00:43:06.149
Look at that extra pi bond,
not animated this time.

00:43:06.149 --> 00:43:09.619
And, of course, you can go
on and you could do see C2H2,

00:43:09.619 --> 00:43:12.055
and you can imagine what
happens in this case,

00:43:12.055 --> 00:43:14.357
is what you would expect.

00:43:14.357 --> 00:43:15.525
Is what you would expect.

00:43:15.525 --> 00:43:17.060
Because, now, I've only got--

00:43:17.060 --> 00:43:21.698
see, each of these carbon atoms
only needs two sigma bonds.

00:43:21.698 --> 00:43:24.167
One to the hydrogen and
one to the other carbon.

00:43:24.167 --> 00:43:26.803
So it's only going to grab one--

00:43:26.803 --> 00:43:28.204
it's only going to hybridize--

00:43:28.204 --> 00:43:30.006
to make those two
equivalent signal bonds,

00:43:30.006 --> 00:43:32.709
it's only going to hybridize
with one of the p electrons.

00:43:32.709 --> 00:43:34.711
So that's sp.

00:43:34.711 --> 00:43:36.212
That's sp hybridization.

00:43:36.212 --> 00:43:38.915
But, notice, that in
C2H2, it-- you know,

00:43:38.915 --> 00:43:45.622
now, I've got 2p orbitals
left over on each carbon atom.

00:43:45.622 --> 00:43:49.859
And so you can imagine that
with these 2p orbitals,

00:43:49.859 --> 00:43:51.761
well, you know this
already, right.

00:43:51.761 --> 00:43:54.230
When-- when we talked
about p orbitals,

00:43:54.230 --> 00:43:57.233
we talked about
how, you know, you

00:43:57.233 --> 00:43:58.935
might-- you know,
you've got this, right.

00:43:58.935 --> 00:43:59.402
Awe.

00:44:01.938 --> 00:44:02.405
OK.

00:44:02.405 --> 00:44:04.007
That's supposed to come
like out of the plane

00:44:04.007 --> 00:44:04.774
and into the plane.

00:44:04.774 --> 00:44:06.042
They're orthogonal.

00:44:06.042 --> 00:44:07.043
They're orthogonal.

00:44:07.043 --> 00:44:07.510
Right.

00:44:07.510 --> 00:44:14.784
And so-- so two of them are
coming at the other atom.

00:44:14.784 --> 00:44:17.687
One is like this, and
the other's like that.

00:44:17.687 --> 00:44:19.789
And then the other atom
is one like this and one--

00:44:19.789 --> 00:44:22.525
well, it might not at first,
but then when it sees those,

00:44:22.525 --> 00:44:23.760
it's like, hey, wait a second.

00:44:23.760 --> 00:44:27.697
If I do this, we can pi bond.

00:44:27.697 --> 00:44:28.231
Right.

00:44:28.231 --> 00:44:30.333
The other ones are like
this, and they pi bond,

00:44:30.333 --> 00:44:33.236
and so you get a carbon
carbon triple bond.

00:44:33.236 --> 00:44:35.038
You get a carbon
carbon triple bond

00:44:35.038 --> 00:44:38.908
for a C2H2 with
sp hybridization.

00:44:38.908 --> 00:44:40.610
Now, it's not-- oh, there it is.

00:44:40.610 --> 00:44:41.077
Right.

00:44:41.077 --> 00:44:41.911
OK.

00:44:41.911 --> 00:44:44.280
Forms pipe bond network,
forms sigma bond network.

00:44:44.280 --> 00:44:46.449
It's not just carbon
that hybridizes.

00:44:46.449 --> 00:44:49.285
It's not-- carbon is
a classic example.

00:44:49.285 --> 00:44:56.559
But, you know, if you look
